# S12.1 r6 legacy-owner orphan recovery diagnosis

## Observed stop

The sole r6 operator invocation authenticated successfully and stopped in the
canonical pre-deployment orphan recovery with `S12_1_WORKTREE_LAYOUT_RED`.
Preflight, fixed release-input installation, backup and deployment did not
begin. Wrapper cleanup removed the trusted controller, fixed inputs and private
upload directory.

## Deterministic cause

The pristine V1 journal records unchanged `chatops:chatops` repository roots
and Git directories. A read-only traversal found no multiply-linked regular
files. It did find pre-existing nested ownership created by historical
privileged validation/runtime activity: 105 Application worktree entries, 12
Control worktree entries and 29 Control Git entries are `root:chatops` or
`root:root`. Representative classes are Python bytecode caches, validation
state, historical analysis material and Git objects/refs.

The r6 pristine-release predicate called the strict non-transition worktree
validator. That validator permits only the recorded repository owner for every
nested entry, so it cannot recognize the canonical server posture even though
the V1 journal is pristine and direct release performs no recursive ownership
mutation.

## Minimal correction

Use the validator's existing journal-transition owner set only inside the
pristine V1 orphan predicate. The accepted nested owners remain exactly the
recorded owner/group, root with the recorded group, or root/root. Exact
repository-root and Git-directory metadata are compared before this call.
Single-link regular files, canonical Git/index identity, structurally valid and
fingerprinted POSIX ACLs, active-handle checks, filesystem-event guards and
durable journal release remain fail-closed.

No manual journal, Git metadata, ACL or ownership repair is authorized or
performed.
